course: dictionary
lesson: 988
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists them as 1), 2) and 3). To find the right one, look at the context: the words and sentences around the word. «Oliver's nan calls police «bobbies» after Robert Peel, who set up London's police.» The clues are police and London, so bobbies must mean police officers.
explain: A capital letter is a clue too. Bobby with a capital B is a name, like a friend in your class called Bobby. When you see a name, the word is not talking about police or hair grips.
explain: Words can also carry history. In 1829 Robert Peel set up London's police, and the officers were nicknamed «bobbies» after him. So «a bobby on the beat» means a police officer walking the streets. In America and Canada, a bobby pin is a small hair grip, so the same spelling can mean something quite different. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Bobby has three meanings: a police officer (UK, old-fashioned and informal), a boy's or girl's name, and a bobby pin (US and Canada).
fact: «Bobby» with a capital B is a name, short for Robert or Roberta.
fact: «A bobby on the beat» means a police officer walking the streets.
fact: The police nickname comes from Sir Robert Peel, who set up the London police in 1829.
fact: A bobby pin is a small hair grip, used in America and Canada.
exercise: «Oliver's nan calls police «bobbies» after Robert Peel.» What does bobbies mean here? | *Police officers | Boys called Robert | Small hair grips | Hats for walking in the rain
exercise: Sam's little brother is called Bobby. Which meaning fits? | *A boy's name, short for Robert | A small hair grip | A street in London | A police officer
exercise: Lily clipped her fringe back with a bobby pin. What is a bobby pin? | *A small hair grip, used in America and Canada | A police officer on the beat | A nickname for a boy | A name for a girl
test: «A bobby on the beat walked slowly past the shops.» What is a bobby here? | A shop worker | A hair grip | *A police officer | A boy called Bobby
test: «Bobby came to tea and brought a cake.» Which meaning fits best? | A bobby pin | *A name for a boy or girl | A bobby on the beat | A police officer
exam: Which clue in «officers were nicknamed «bobbies» after him» shows that bobbies means police officers? | A nickname is a funny name | The name Robert is a boy's name | *The words officers and police in the same story | A bobby pin is a hair grip
